# /etc/rc -- the boot script, run by /etc/init as sh /etc/rc.

date >/dev/console
/etc/update

# WHAT THE DATE SAYS, AND WHAT SET IT.  This machine has no clock-calendar a
# program can read (include/sys/param.h), so main.c's iinit() seeds `time' from
# the root superblock's s_time -- which b6fsutil stamped with -T ROOTTIME when it
# built the image (kernel/test/CMakeLists.txt), 2026-07-25 08:23:00 GMT.  That is
# the epoch and not the ticking: the interval timer runs the clock forward in
# real seconds either way.  The OPERATOR replaces the epoch with `date
# yymmddhhmm' in single-user mode, before the ^D that gets here, which is v7's
# own answer on a machine with no battery clock and is what this line then
# prints.  kernel/date.ini is that operator under SIMH; kernel/README.md,
# "Telling it the date", is the account, and cmd/at/README.md is why it was
# written (task C21).
#
# WHICH TEST SEES THIS FILE is kernel/test/multi and only that one.  /etc/rc does
# not run until the first shell has exited (cmd/init/init.c: shutdown, single,
# runcom, multiple), and multi.ini is the one test that types the ^D that gets
# there; kernel/test/boot quits on the first prompt, with the disk still attached
# read-only, and kernel/test/core stays in single-user to mount the test pack.
# multi asserts BOTH lines: its expect matches the year its own stage 1 set, with
# the seconds left out because they move, and once both users are logged in it
# asks `ps -ax' for the daemon below.
#
# THE UPDATE LINE IS A DAEMON IN A SCRIPT INIT RE-RUNS, and that is safe rather
# than lucky (task C20; cmd/update/README.md is the argument).  init's loop is
# shutdown, single, runcom, merge, multiple -- shutdown() comes FIRST on every
# pass and sweeps the machine with kill(-1, SIGKILL), which kernel/sys4.c grants
# to root for every process but proc[0] and proc[1].  So the copy this line
# started is dead before the line is read again, and only one ever runs.  No `&':
# update forks and the parent exits, which is what keeps runcom()'s wait() from
# holding the boot.  It prints nothing, so it needs no redirection either.
#
# CRON IS THE ONE STILL WAITING ON A PROGRAM (task C22), and that is now all it
# waits on: the clock it was also said to be blocked on was never missing, which
# is what task C21 established (cmd/at/README.md).  Each line comes back as its
# command is ported.  Note for whoever writes it that cron's own setuid(1) and
# atrun's setuid(2) do not compose as they stand -- cmd/TODO.md C22 has it.
#
# ACCTON WAS THE THIRD AND IS NOT COMING.  Process accounting is in the "Not
# ported, and why" table of cmd/TODO.md now, not in a task: the kernel side is
# real -- acct(2) works -- but there is nobody on this machine to bill, sa's
# whole subject is a record nothing here writes, and it would want a /usr/adm
# that root.manifest does not have.  So that line of the v7 rc is gone rather
# than pending.
#
# MOUNT IS NO LONGER ONE OF THEM AND STILL GETS NO LINE.  Task C4f put
# /etc/mount and /etc/umount on the image, but a boot image has nothing to
# mount: one EC-5052 drive is the whole of the store, /usr is a directory of
# the root rather than a volume of its own, and swap lives on the drums.  The
# second and third drives exist only in kernel/test/mount, which attaches them
# itself.  A mount line here would fail on every real boot.
#
# FSCK IS THE EXCEPTION, and it is C4d's one loose end: /etc/fsck exists now and
# `/etc/fsck -y /dev/rmd0 >/dev/console' would be the v7 line.  It is not here
# for the same reason the rm below is not -- a line in this file has exactly one
# home for its assertion, kernel/test/console, which is DISABLED (kernel task
# 35).  Note also that a fsck which REPAIRS the mounted root does not return:
# it prints BOOT UNIX (NO SYNC!) and spins, deliberately, because the kernel's
# in-core superblock would otherwise undo the repair.  So whoever adds the line
# adds it knowing that a damaged root stops the boot dead, which is what it is
# supposed to do and is not what an unattended test wants.
#
# rm left that list with task C1b, and so did cp, ln and mv -- but the only
# line of the v7 rc that used rm cleared /tmp of an editor's leftovers, and
# THE EDITOR HAS ARRIVED: task C3 put ed on the image, whose temp file is
# /tmp/e<pid><letter>, and /etc/motd has always claimed that /tmp is cleared at
# boot.  So `rm -f /tmp/*' is a line this file now wants.
#
# What it is waiting on is not ed but an ASSERTION.  cmd/README.md SS7 step 4
# gives a line in this script exactly one home for one -- kernel/test/console,
# the only test that types the ^D that gets here -- and console is DISABLED
# (kernel/TODO.md task 35).  An unasserted line in the boot script that three
# enabled tests walk through is worse than an honest deferral, so the line
# comes back with task 35 and not before.  Note also that `rm -f' is silent on
# a missing name and on an unmatched glob but NOT on a directory (cmd/rm/rm.c
# prints for that whatever -f says), and kernel/test/session leaves /tmp/d
# behind deliberately -- so whoever adds the line redirects it, or checks that
# every test reaching /etc/rc leaves /tmp free of directories first.
#
# TWO MORE LINES OF THE v7 rc COULD RUN TODAY AND DELIBERATELY DO NOT.
# `cat /dev/null >/etc/utmp' is redundant rather than premature now: /etc/utmp
# IS read and written since kernel task 29b -- login writes a record and getty's
# death blanks it -- but init's own merge() truncates the file with creat() on
# every pass into multi-user, which happens after this script and does the same
# job.  A line here would only write the root filesystem twice per boot.
# `PATH=/bin:/usr/bin' would set what the shell already uses: its own default
# path is ":/bin:/usr/bin" (cmd/sh/msg.c), and both directories now exist -- task
# C9a put /usr/bin/cpp on the image.  So the line would name the same two
# directories the shell searches anyway, and only drop the leading empty element
# that makes the CURRENT directory first.  (login exports that same string to
# every user, for a different reason: it is v7's, kept verbatim.)
